         "0",
         "scicode.11",
         "Given $j$ and $d$, write a function that returns a standard basis vector $|j\\rangle$ in $d$-dimensional space. If $d$ is given as an int and $j$ is given as a list $[j_1,j_2\\cdots,j_n]$, then return the tensor product $|j_1\\rangle|j_2\\rangle\\cdots|j_n\\rangle$ of $d$-dimensional basis vectors. If $d$ is also given as a list $[d_1,d_2,\\cdots,d_n]$, return $|j_1\\rangle|j_2\\rangle\\cdots|j_n\\rangle$ as tensor product of $d_1$, $d_2$, ..., and $d_n$ dimensional basis vectors.\n\ndef ket(dim, *args):\n    '''Input:\n    dim: int or list, dimension of the ket\n    args: int or list, the i-th basis vector\n    Output:\n    out: dim dimensional array of float, the matrix representation of the ket\n    '''\n    if isinstance(dim, int):\n        if len(args) == 1 and isinstance(args[0], int):\n            j = args[0]\n            out = np.zeros(dim, dtype=float)\n            out[j] = 1.0\n            return out\n        elif len(args) == 1 and isinstance(args[0], list):\n            j_list = args[0]\n            out = np.array([1.0])\n            for j in j_list:\n                basis = np.zeros(dim, dtype=float)\n                basis[j] = 1.0\n                out = np.kron(out, basis)\n            return out\n    elif isinstance(dim, list):\n        if len(args) == 1 and isinstance(args[0], list):\n            j_list = args[0]\n            if len(dim) != len(j_list):\n                raise ValueError(\"Length of dim and j must match\")\n            out = np.array([1.0])\n            for d, j in zip(dim, j_list):\n                basis = np.zeros(d, dtype=float)\n                basis[j] = 1.0\n                out = np.kron(out, basis)\n            return out\n    raise ValueError(\"Invalid input types or dimensions\")\n\n------\n\nUsing the ket function, write a function that generates a bipartite maximally entangled state where both parties are encoded by $m$-rail encoding.\n\ndef multi_rail_encoding_state(rails):\n    '''Returns the density matrix of the multi-rail encoding state\n    Input:\n    rails: int, number of rails\n    Output:\n    state: 2**(2*rails) x 2**(2*rails) dimensional array of numpy.float64 type\n    '''\n    dim = 2**rails\n    state = np.zeros((dim**2, dim**2), dtype=np.float64)\n    \n    # Generate all possible basis states for the first party\n    basis_states = list(itertools.product([0, 1], repeat=rails))\n    \n    for b in basis_states:\n        # Create the ket for the first party\n        ket_a = ket(dim, list(b))\n        # Create the corresponding ket for the second party (same as first party)\n        ket_b = ket(dim, list(b))\n        # Tensor product to get the combined state\n        combined_ket = np.kron(ket_a, ket_b)\n        # Outer product to get the density matrix contribution\n        state += np.outer(combined_ket, combined_ket)\n    \n    # Normalize the state\n    state /= dim\n    \n    return state\n\n------\n\nWrite a function that returns the tensor product of an arbitrary number of matrices/vectors.\n\ndef tensor(*args):\n    '''Takes the tensor product of an arbitrary number of matrices/vectors.\n    Input:\n    args: any number of nd arrays of floats, corresponding to input matrices\n    Output:\n    M: the tensor product (kronecker product) of input matrices, 2d array of floats\n    '''\n    if len(args) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"At least one input matrix/vector is required\")\n    \n    M = args[0]\n    for mat in args[1:]:\n        M = np.kron(M, mat)\n    \n    return M\n\n------\n\nWrite a function that applies the Kraus operators of a quantum channel on subsystems of a state with tensor function. If sys and dim are given as None, then the channel acts on the entire system of the state rho. If sys is given as a list, then the channel is applied to each subsystem in that list, and the dimension of each subsystem also must be given.\n\ndef apply_channel(K, rho, sys=None, dim=None):\n    '''Applies the channel with Kraus operators in K to the state rho on\n    systems specified by the list sys. The dimensions of the subsystems of\n    rho are given by dim.\n    Inputs:\n    K: list of 2d array of floats, list of Kraus operators\n    rho: 2d array of floats, input density matrix\n    sys: list of int or None, list of subsystems to apply the channel, None means full system\n    dim: list of int or None, list of dimensions of each subsystem, None means full system\n    Output:\n    matrix: output density matrix of floats\n    '''\n    if sys is None and dim is None:\n        # Apply channel to the entire system\n        matrix = np.zeros_like(rho, dtype=np.float64)\n        for k in K:\n            matrix += k @ rho @ k.conj().T\n        return matrix\n    elif sys is not None and dim is not None:\n        if len(sys) != len(dim):\n            raise ValueError(\"Length of sys and dim must match\")\n        \n        total_dim = np.prod(dim)\n        if rho.shape[0] != total_dim or rho.shape[1] != total_dim:\n            raise ValueError(\"Dimensions of rho do not match the product of subsystem dimensions\")\n        \n        # Create identity operators for subsystems not in sys\n        id_ops = []\n        current_sys = 0\n        for d in dim:\n            if current_sys in sys:\n                id_ops.append(None)  # Placeholder for Kraus operators\n            else:\n                id_ops.append(np.eye(d))\n            current_sys += 1\n        \n        matrix = np.zeros_like(rho, dtype=np.float64)\n        for k in K:\n            # Construct the full operator by tensoring with identities\n            full_op = None\n            current_sys = 0\n            for i, d in enumerate(dim):\n                if current_sys in sys:\n                    op_part = k\n                else:\n                    op_part = id_ops[i]\n                \n                if full_op is None:\n                    full_op = op_part\n                else:\n                    full_op = np.kron(full_op, op_part)\n                current_sys += 1\n            \n            matrix += full_op @ rho @ full_op.conj().T\n        \n        return matrix\n    else:\n        raise ValueError(\"Both sys and dim must be None or both must be provided\")\n\n------\n\nWrite a function that returns the Kraus operators of generalized amplitude damping channels parametrized by $\\gamma$ and $N$.\n\ndef generalized_amplitude_damping_channel(gamma, N):\n    '''Generates the generalized amplitude damping channel.\n    Inputs:\n    gamma: float, damping parameter\n    N: float, thermal parameter\n    Output:\n    kraus: list of Kraus operators as 2x2 arrays of floats, [A1, A2, A3, A4]\n    '''\n    A1 = np.sqrt(gamma * (N + 1)) * np.array([[1, 0], [0, np.sqrt(1 - gamma)]], dtype=np.float64)\n    A2 = np.sqrt(gamma * (N + 1)) * np.array([[0, np.sqrt(gamma)], [0, 0]], dtype=np.float64)\n    A3 = np.sqrt(gamma * N) * np.array([[np.sqrt(1 - gamma), 0], [0, 1]], dtype=np.float64)\n    A4 = np.sqrt(gamma * N) * np.array([[0, 0], [np.sqrt(gamma), 0]], dtype=np.float64)\n    \n    kraus = [A1, A2, A3, A4]\n    return kraus\n\n------\n\nWrite a function with and functions that returns the output of sending the $m$-rail encoded state through $m$ generalized amplitude damping channels $\\mathcal{A}_{\\gamma_1,N_1}$ to receiver 1 and $m$ generalized amplitude damping channels $\\mathcal{A}_{\\gamma_2,N_2}$ to receiver function.\n\ndef output_state(rails, gamma_1, N_1, gamma_2, N_2):\n    '''Inputs:\n    rails: int, number of rails\n    gamma_1: float, damping parameter of the first channel\n    N_1: float, thermal parameter of the first channel\n    gamma_2: float, damping parameter of the second channel\n    N_2: float, thermal parameter of the second channel\n    Output\n    state: 2**(2*rails) x 2**(2*rails) dimensional array of floats, the output state\n    '''\n    # Generate the initial multi-rail encoded state\n    initial_state = multi_rail_encoding_state(rails)\n    \n    # Generate Kraus operators for each channel\n    kraus_1 = generalized_amplitude_damping_channel(gamma_1, N_1)\n    kraus_2 = generalized_amplitude_damping_channel(gamma_2, N_2)\n    \n    # Define the subsystems and dimensions\n    dim = [2] * (2 * rails)  # Each rail is a qubit (dimension 2)\n    sys_1 = list(range(rails))  # First m rails (receiver 1)\n    sys_2 = list(range(rails, 2 * rails))  # Next m rails (receiver 2)\n    \n    # Apply the first set of channels to receiver 1's rails\n    state_after_1 = apply_channel(kraus_1, initial_state, sys=sys_1, dim=dim)\n    \n    # Apply the second set of channels to receiver 2's rails\n    state = apply_channel(kraus_2, state_after_1, sys=sys_2, dim=dim)\n    \n    return state\n\n------\n\nEach of the two receivers measure whether the $m$ qubits are in the one-particle sector, i.e., whether there are $m-1$ 0's and one Write a function that returns the corresponding global projector.\n\ndef measurement(rails):\n    '''Returns the measurement projector\n    Input:\n    rails: int, number of rails\n    Output:\n    global_proj: ( 2**(2*rails), 2**(2*rails) ) dimensional array of floats\n    '''\n    # Initialize the global projector as a zero matrix\n    dim = 2**(2 * rails)\n    global_proj = np.zeros((dim, dim), dtype=np.float64)\n    \n    # Generate all possible basis states for the combined system\n    basis_states = list(itertools.product([0, 1], repeat=2 * rails))\n    \n    for b in basis_states:\n        # Check if the state is in the one-particle sector for both receivers\n        # First m qubits (receiver 1) should have exactly one 1\n        if sum(b[:rails]) == 1:\n            # Next m qubits (receiver 2) should have exactly one 1\n            if sum(b[rails:]) == 1:\n                # Create the corresponding basis ket\n                ket_state = ket([2] * (2 * rails), list(b))\n                # Add the outer product to the projector\n                global_proj += np.outer(ket_state, ket_state)\n    \n    return global_proj\n\n------\n\nPermute the subsystems of a state according to the order specified. The dimensions of subsystems are also given as input.\n\ndef syspermute(X, perm, dim):\n    '''Permutes order of subsystems in the multipartite operator X.\n    Inputs:\n    X: 2d array of floats with equal dimensions, the density matrix of the state\n    perm: list of int containing the desired order\n    dim: list of int containing the dimensions of all subsystems.\n    Output:\n    Y: 2d array of floats with equal dimensions, the density matrix of the permuted state\n    '''\n    if len(perm) != len(dim):\n        raise ValueError(\"Length of perm and dim must match\")\n    \n    # Total number of subsystems\n    n_sys = len(dim)\n    \n    # Check if perm is a valid permutation\n    if sorted(perm) != list(range(n_sys)):\n        raise ValueError(\"perm must be a valid permutation of subsystem indices\")\n    \n    # Reshape X into a tensor with indices for each subsystem\n    # The shape is (d1, d1, d2, d2, ..., dn, dn)\n    tensor_shape = []\n    for d in dim:\n        tensor_shape.extend([d, d])\n    X_tensor = X.reshape(tensor_shape)\n    \n    # Prepare the permutation for the reshaped tensor\n    # Each subsystem has two indices (row and column)\n    # So the permutation for the tensor is [2*p, 2*p+1 for p in perm]\n    tensor_perm = []\n    for p in perm:\n        tensor_perm.extend([2*p, 2*p + 1])\n    \n    # Apply the permutation\n    Y_tensor = np.transpose(X_tensor, axes=tensor_perm)\n    \n    # Reshape back to matrix form\n    Y = Y_tensor.reshape((X.shape[0], X.shape[1]))\n    \n    return Y\n\n------\n\nCalculate the partial trace of a state, tracing out a list of subsystems. Dimensions of all subsystems are given as inputs. Use the syspermute function.\n\ndef partial_trace(X, sys, dim):\n    '''Inputs:\n    X: 2d array of floats with equal dimensions, the density matrix of the state\n    sys: list of int containing systems over which to take the partial trace (i.e., the systems to discard).\n    dim: list of int containing dimensions of all subsystems.\n    Output:\n    2d array of floats with equal dimensions, density matrix after partial trace.\n    '''\n    if len(sys) == 0:\n        return X\n    \n    # Create the permutation to move the traced-out systems to the end\n    n_sys = len(dim)\n    remaining_sys = [i for i in range(n_sys) if i not in sys]\n    perm = remaining_sys + sys\n    \n    # Permute the subsystems\n    X_perm = syspermute(X, perm, dim)\n    \n    # Calculate the new dimensions after permutation\n    kept_dim = [dim[i] for i in remaining_sys]\n    traced_dim = [dim[i] for i in sys]\n    \n    # Reshape the matrix into a tensor with indices for kept and traced systems\n    # Shape: (d_kept1, d_kept1, ..., d_keptK, d_keptK, d_trace1, d_trace1, ..., d_traceT, d_traceT)\n    tensor_shape = []\n    for d in kept_dim:\n        tensor_shape.extend([d, d])\n    for d in traced_dim:\n        tensor_shape.extend([d, d])\n    X_tensor = X_perm.reshape(tensor_shape)\n    \n    # Calculate the partial trace by summing over the traced systems' indices\n    # The traced systems are the last len(traced_dim) * 2 dimensions\n    trace_axes = list(range(len(kept_dim) * 2, len(tensor_shape), 2))\n    Y_tensor = np.trace(X_tensor, axis1=trace_axes[0], axis2=trace_axes[0] + 1)\n    \n    # Reshape back to matrix form\n    Y = Y_tensor.reshape((np.prod(kept_dim), np.prod(kept_dim)))\n    \n    return Y\n\n------\n\nCalculate the von Neumann entropy of a state with log base 2\n\ndef entropy(rho):\n    '''Inputs:\n    rho: 2d array of floats with equal dimensions, the density matrix of the state\n    Output:\n    en: quantum (von Neumann) entropy of the state rho, float\n    '''\n    # Compute the eigenvalues of the density matrix\n    eigvals = scipy.linalg.eigvalsh(rho)\n    \n    # Remove any negative eigenvalues due to numerical errors\n    eigvals = np.maximum(eigvals, 0)\n    \n    # Normalize the eigenvalues to sum to 1 (in case of numerical errors)\n    eigvals = eigvals / np.sum(eigvals)\n    \n    # Compute the entropy by summing -p*log2(p) for non-zero p\n    en = 0.0\n    for p in eigvals:\n        if p > 0:\n            en -= p * np.log2(p)\n    \n    return en\n\n------\n\nCalculate the coherent information of a state with and function.\n\ndef coherent_inf_state(rho_AB, dimA, dimB):\n    '''Inputs:\n    rho_AB: 2d array of floats with equal dimensions, the state we evaluate coherent information\n    dimA: int, dimension of system A\n    dimB: int, dimension of system B\n    Output\n    co_inf: float, the coherent information of the state rho_AB\n    '''\n    # Calculate the entropy of system A\n    rho_A = partial_trace(rho_AB, [1], [dimA, dimB])\n    S_A = entropy(rho_A)\n    \n    # Calculate the entropy of the joint system AB\n    S_AB = entropy(rho_AB)\n    \n    # Calculate the coherent information: I(A⟩B) = S(B) - S(AB)\n    # First, get rho_B by tracing out system A\n    rho_B = partial_trace(rho_AB, [0], [dimA, dimB])\n    S_B = entropy(rho_B)\n    \n    co_inf = S_B - S_AB\n    \n    return co_inf\n\n\nWe will perform the hashing protocol on the post-selected state after the measurement in . The amount of entanglement produced by the hashing protocol per state is the coherent information of the state. Calculate the rate of entanglement per channel with function , and .\n\ndef rate(rails, gamma_1, N_1, gamma_2, N_2):\n    '''Inputs:\n    rails: int, number of rails\n    gamma_1: float, damping parameter of the first channel\n    N_1: float, thermal parameter of the first channel\n    gamma_2: float, damping parameter of the second channel\n    N_2: float, thermal parameter of the second channel\n    Output: float, the achievable rate of our protocol\n    '''\n\n    return rate\n\nDEPENDENCIES:\nUse only the following dependencies in your solution. Do not include these dependencies at the beginning of your code.\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport itertools\nimport scipy.linalg",
         "[-0.02709912  0.00688955  0.00361974 ...  0.00525369 -0.01638506\n  0.00452549]"
